'''tribunicial, tribunitial''' (trib-ụ̄-nish′a̤l), ''a.'' [< L. ''tribunicius, tribunitius,'' of or belonging to a tribune, < ''tribunus,'' a tribune: see ''tribune''<sup>1</sup>.] Pertaining to or befitting a tribune; characteristic of a tribune or of his power or functions.

{{smaller block|My lord Sejanus<br>Is to receive this day in open senate<br>The ''tribunitial'' dignity.{{right|''B. Jonson,'' Sejanus, v. 7.}}

This insolent ''tribunitial'' veto has long encumbered all our public affairs.{{right|''B. Franklin,'' Autobiog., p. 331.}}}}

'''tribunician, tribunitian''' (trib-ụ̄-nish′a̤n), ''a.'' [= F. ''tribunitien'' (cf. It. ''tribunizio'' = Sp. ''tribunicio''), < L. ''tribunicius, tribunitius,'' of or belonging to a tribune, < ''tribunus,'' a tribune: see ''tribune''<sup>1</sup>.] Same as ''tribunicial.''

{{smaller block|The title of the ''tribunician'' power connected the monarch with the interest of the lower orders.{{right|''W. W. Capes,'' The Early Empire, i.}}}}

'''tribunicious†, tribunitious†''' (trib-ụ̄-nish′us), ''a.'' [< L. ''tribunicius, tribunitius,'' of or belonging to a tribune: see ''tribunicial.''] Same as ''tribunicial.''

{{smaller block|Let them not come in multitudes, or in a ''tribunitious'' manner, for that is to clamour councils, not to inform them.{{right|''Bacon,'' Counsel (ed. 1887).}}}}

'''tribus''' (trī′bus), ''n.''; pl. ''tribus.'' [NL.: see ''tribe.''] In ''zoöl.'' and ''bot.,'' a tribe as a classificatory group.

'''tributarily''' (trib′ụ̄-tạ̄-ri-li), ''adv.'' In a tributary manner.

